              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Depending on the specific type of drug, opioids are either derived naturally from the **opium poppy plant** (*Papaver somniferum*) or created chemically in a **laboratory** to mimic the plant's compounds. \n\nBecause of these differences in origin, opioids are generally classified into three categories:\n\n### 1. Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nThese are chemical compounds extracted directly from the natural plant matter of the opium poppy. To harvest them, growers make shallow cuts in the unripe seed pods of the poppy plant, allowing a sticky, milky sap (latex) to seep out. Once dried, this sap is collected and refined.\n*   **Examples:** Morphine, codeine, and thebaine.\n\n### 2. Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nThese drugs are created in a laboratory by taking naturally occurring opiates (such as morphine or thebaine) and chemically altering their molecular structures to change how they act on the body. \n*   **Examples:** Heroin (made from morphine), oxycodone (OxyContin, Percocet), hydrocodone (Vicodin), hydromorphone (Dilaudid), and buprenorphine.\n\n### 3. Synthetic Opioids\nThese are entirely man-made in a laboratory and do not use any part of the physical poppy plant. Scientists design these chemical compounds from scratch to mimic the molecular structure of natural opiates so that they bind to the exact same pain receptors in the human brain and nervous system. Because they are engineered, some synthetic opioids can be manufactured to be extraordinarily potent.\n*   **Examples:** Fentanyl, methadone, tramadol, and pethidine (Demerol).\n\n### How They Work\nRegardless of whether they are grown in a field or synthesized in a test tube, all opioids work by binding to specific **opioid receptors** on nerve cells in the brain, spinal cord, and gastrointestinal tract. By attaching to these receptors, they block pain signals sent from the body to the brain and release large amounts of dopamine, which produces temporary feelings of intense relaxation and euphoria. This dual effect is what makes them highly effective for severe pain management, but also makes them highly addictive.",
